+# Decision: Self-hosted WebSocket delivery of committed changes
+
+## Context
+
+Depends on the "Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event" decision (see the HLD's Key Design Decisions): every committed edit is one authoritative event revision, and delivery is a transport problem, not a consistency problem.
+
+Committed board changes must reach connected browsers fast enough to feel live during an event: 800 simultaneous staff browsers, opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second, with subsecond updates desirable and 15 seconds an absolute freshness floor. The board must not be coupled to anything that breaks if a vendor changes its terms, and operating cost must stay predictable as the pilot grows across forty markets.
+
+If this decision is wrong, the cost shows up either as recurring vendor fees baked into every market's board, or as connection infrastructure we cannot keep alive during opening-time bursts — both expensive to reverse once the three downstream nodes (change store, delivery, browser sync) are sized around the choice.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates** (binary; failing an option eliminates it):
+
+- **Meet the freshness floor.** Every option must deliver committed changes within 15 seconds under normal connectivity, and must degrade to catch-up recovery for browsers disconnected up to 20 minutes.
+- **No customer data in the payload.** Per the tenets, updates contain stall IDs and operational statuses only — this permits self-hosted infrastructure to carry operational data without adding a data-protection regime.
+
+**Weighted criteria:**
+
+- **Update latency (major).** How quickly committed revisions arrive at connected browsers. Tenet-driven: the board's purpose is a shared, live view of stall status.
+- **Vendor coupling (major).** How much of the delivery mechanism depends on an external provider's availability, pricing, and protocol. Tenet-driven: self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted, but nothing in the project's intent commits the board to a realtime vendor.
+- **Operational burden (moderate).** Connection capacity planning, session lifecycle, reconnect handling, and reliability engineering the team owns as a result.
+- **Recurring cost (moderate).** Fees that scale with browser count and message volume.
+- **Implementation simplicity (minor).** How much code and state the route requires beyond the shared HTTPS command boundary.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Each browser polls a cursor-based HTTP endpoint every five seconds; the server returns revisions newer than the browser's cursor. The change store needs cursor queries over its revision history. Delivery stays plain request/response over the existing HTTPS boundary — no connection state at all. Browsers run a simple polling loop with no connection lifecycle to manage.
+
+- Meet the freshness floor: **passes** — five seconds is within the 15-second target; catch-up is just a query from a new cursor.
+- No customer data in the payload: **passes**.
+- Update latency: **weak** — every change waits up to the polling interval; the board lags a few seconds by construction.
+- Vendor coupling: **strong** — nothing external.
+- Operational burden: **strong** — stateless endpoints only; capacity is ordinary HTTP scaling.
+- Recurring cost: **strong** — no vendor.
+- Implementation simplicity: **strong** — the simplest of the three; but at 800 browsers polling every five seconds, most reads return nothing, and the read volume grows with browser count rather than with edit volume.
+
+Summary: minimal machinery, at the price of a permanent few-second lag and read amplification that scales with audience size.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+The delivery component operates its own WebSocket connections. Each browser opens one socket session; committed revisions are pushed over the connection as they happen. The change store keeps replayable revisions so a browser that reconnects can catch up from the last revision it received. Delivery owns session lifecycle, capacity (800 concurrent connections), and reliable publication; browsers carry reconnect and catch-up logic.
+
+- Meet the freshness floor: **passes** — push delivers subsecond; catch-up covers disconnections.
+- No customer data in the payload: **passes**.
+- Update latency: **strong** — subsecond push under normal connectivity.
+- Vendor coupling: **strong** — nothing external.
+- Operational burden: **weak** — the team owns connection capacity planning, session management, and reconnect/replay reliability.
+- Recurring cost: **strong** — no vendor fees.
+- Implementation simplicity: **weak** — connection state in delivery, replay in the store, reconnect logic in every browser.
+
+Summary: subsecond updates with no vendor, paid for in connection and reconnect operations the team owns outright.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+A hosted provider (e.g. a managed pub/sub-to-browser service) operates the connections; our delivery component publishes each committed revision to the provider, and browsers subscribe through its SDK and subscription model. The change store needs publication reconciliation — a revision published to the provider is not confirmed until we can verify delivery obligations are met. Provider credentials and account management become part of deployment.
+
+- Meet the freshness floor: **passes** — managed push with its own reconnect handling.
+- No customer data in the payload: **passes**.
+- Update latency: **strong** — provider-grade push.
+- Vendor coupling: **weak** — the board's live path depends on the provider's protocol, availability, and terms.
+- Operational burden: **partial** — no connection infrastructure to run, but reconciliation and provider ops are added.
+- Recurring cost: **weak** — fees scale with browser count and message volume, forever.
+- Implementation simplicity: **strong** — fastest route to low latency; least code on the browser side.
+
+Summary: lowest connection-ops burden and quickest to low latency, at the price of recurring fees and permanent provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+Self-hosted WebSockets were selected. The deciding criteria are the major ones: update latency and vendor coupling. Self-hosted WebSockets are the only option strong on both — polling gives up the latency a live board wants, and the hosted service gives up vendor independence. The price is owned connection operations, which the tenets permit (self-hosted infrastructure is allowed, and the payload's stall-IDs-and-statuses content keeps the self-hosted route free of a data-protection regime).
+
+Committing forecloses the vendor-assisted path and obligates the downstream nodes: the change store must keep replayable revisions sufficient for reconnect catch-up, delivery must manage socket sessions and reliable publication, and browser sync must carry reconnect and catch-up behavior. These obligations are downstream design work, not settled here.
+
+A decision doc (this file) was kept rather than a table row alone because the tradeoff spans all three downstream nodes and a cold reader sizing them could plausibly re-litigate it.
