 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Browsers subscribe over WebSocket connections our delivery component operates; the change store keeps replayable revisions for reconnect catch-up. Alternatives: five-second HTTPS polling (stateless and simple, but repeated reads at browser scale and seconds of lag behind what sockets provide) and a hosted realtime service (least connection work, but recurring fees and coupling the board to a realtime vendor). We chose to own connection capacity and reconnect handling to get fast updates without vendor coupling; the tenets permit self-hosted infrastructure, and the payload contains only stall IDs and operational statuses. Full tradeoff record: `docs/decisions/self-hosted-socket-delivery.md`.
